**Site Report – BMGS-bori-field, Bori Metropolis**

The VES interpretation yields a 6-layer resistivity model. Resistivity increases sharply from Layer 1 (1005 Ω·m) to a high-resistivity Layer 2 (3588 Ω·m), then decreases through Layer 3 (1358 Ω·m) and further to a low-resistivity Layer 4 (333 Ω·m), before rising again through Layer 5 (2950 Ω·m) to the resistive basal Layer 6 (5469 Ω·m). This sequence of rise–fall–fall–rise–rise produces the segment codes: Layers 1-2-3 = K, 2-3-4 = Q, 3-4-5 = H, 4-5-6 = A, giving the curve type **KQHA**. This matches the classification on file, confirming the interpreted curve type is consistent with the reported layer resistivities. No discrepancies were found between the tabulated resistivity values and the stated curve-type label. The sequence suggests alternating resistive and conductive units, possibly reflecting interbedded sand/clay or weathered-bedrock layering at depth.

